\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Generalizability Theory}
\label{sec:gtheory}

Generalizability theory (G-theory) treats each measurement as one observation from a universe of admissible conditions defined by the crossed facets of an evaluation design \cite{shavelson1991generalizability, brennan2001generalizability}. The \emph{object of measurement} is the entity whose consistency is of interest. In our setting, test items ($p$) serve as the object of measurement, and the goal is to determine how reliably item-level scores generalize across the methodological conditions under which they are collected.

A \emph{G-study} decomposes total observed variance into components $\sigma^2_x$ for each facet $x$ and their interactions. Each component quantifies how much variability that source contributes to the overall measurement. Large interaction components (e.g., $\sigma^2_{pM}$ for item-by-model) indicate that facets do not affect items uniformly but instead create differential difficulty profiles. We estimate variance components via Henderson Method~I, an unbiased ANOVA-based estimator for balanced designs \cite[Ch.~3]{brennan2001generalizability}, with 1,000-resample bootstrap confidence intervals (item-level resampling with replacement, preserving the fully-crossed structure across all other facets) \cite{li2023bootstrap}.

A \emph{D-study} projects the generalizability coefficient under hypothetical designs with different numbers of conditions per facet:
\begin{equation}
G_\text{item} = \frac{\sigma^2_p}{\sigma^2_p + \sigma^2_\text{rel}}
\label{eq:gcoeff}
\end{equation}
where $\sigma^2_\text{rel}$ aggregates all item-by-facet interaction variances, each divided by the number of conditions for the corresponding facets \cite[Ch.~4]{brennan2001generalizability}. This prospective capability distinguishes G-theory from classical ANOVA: rather than only attributing variance post hoc, a D-study enables optimization of evaluation designs \emph{before} data collection \cite{shavelson1989gtheory}. We define \emph{item-related variance} as the sum of the item main effect ($\sigma^2_p$) and the model$\times$item interaction ($\sigma^2_{pM}$). The latter captures differential item functioning (DIF): items whose difficulty ranking changes across models \cite[Ch.~4]{brennan2001generalizability}. Because DIF is operationally an item-level property---it reflects model-specific item difficulty profiles rather than a global model ability shift---aggregating $\sigma^2_p + \sigma^2_{pM}$ provides a unified measure of item-driven variance. We note a semantic difference from educational measurement: person$\times$item DIF reflects unidirectional person responses to items, whereas model$\times$item DIF in LLM evaluation simultaneously reflects architecture-specific capability profiles---a dual interpretation. Bundling $\sigma^2_p + \sigma^2_{pM}$ has psychometric justification \cite[Ch.~4]{brennan2001generalizability}, and we report unbundled components throughout to ensure transparency.

In ML terms, a G-study is a multi-way ANOVA where each facet is a design variable and variance components quantify each variable's contribution; a D-study projects the number of conditions per facet needed for a target reliability $G$, analogous to a signal-to-noise ratio where signal is item difficulty variance and noise is everything that changes across evaluation conditions.

Throughout this paper, we use the following notation: $p$ = item, $M$ = model, $\pi$ = numerical precision, $\delta$ = prompt template, $s$ = random seed, $\tau$ = sampling temperature, $o$ = answer ordering, and $\sigma^2_x$ = variance component for source $x$.

\subsection{Experimental Design: MMLU}
\label{sec:exp001}

We design a fully crossed factorial experiment (exp-001) that simultaneously varies seven facets of LLM evaluation on MMLU. Table~\ref{tab:design_matrix} summarizes the complete design matrix, and Figure~\ref{fig:design_diagram} illustrates the crossing structure.

\paragraph{Facets.} Items ($p$): 200 MMLU questions selected via purposive stratified sampling across 10 of MMLU's 57 subjects (20 per subject, deterministic selection with seed 42). The 10 subjects span four disciplinary domains---STEM: abstract algebra, college physics, high school mathematics, computer security; humanities: world religions, philosophy; social sciences: US foreign policy, international law; professional: professional medicine, clinical knowledge---ensuring that variance estimates are not dominated by a single knowledge domain. Models ($M$): eight open-weight 7--9B parameter instruction-tuned models spanning eight architecture families---DeepSeek-7B, Gemma-2-9B, InternLM2.5-7B, Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct, Mistral-7B-v0.3-Instruct, OLMo-2-7B, Qwen3-8B, and Yi-1.5-9B (full identifiers in Appendix~\ref{app:models})---treated as a quasi-random facet (\S3.6). Numerical precision ($\pi$): BF16, FP16, and FP32, all evaluated with \texttt{enforce\_eager=True} for fair comparison across precision levels. Prompt template ($\delta$): six surface perturbations of the evaluation instruction \cite{sclar2024formatting}, varying option layout (lettered vs.\ numbered), instruction wording (direct vs.\ contextual), and answer prompt cue for MC benchmarks; for FF benchmarks, templates vary chain-of-thought elicitation strategy (full template text in Appendix~\ref{app:models}). Random seed ($s$): six fixed values (42, 123, 456, 789, 1024, 2048). Sampling temperature ($\tau$): three levels (0.0, 0.3, 0.7) spanning deterministic to moderately stochastic decoding. Answer ordering ($o$): four permutations of few-shot demonstration examples (for MMLU; structurally inert for other benchmarks, see \S\ref{sec:exp002}) \cite{pezeshkpour2024ordering}.

\paragraph{Sample sizes.} The cross-model BF16 balanced subset yields 460,800 records (8 models $\times$ 200 items $\times$ 6 prompts $\times$ 6 seeds $\times$ 2 temperatures $\times$ 4 orderings). A single-model analysis (Llama) spans all three precisions, yielding 259,200 binary observations across 1,296 fully balanced conditions. The BF16 subset serves as the primary cross-model sample because BF16 is the standard inference precision for these architectures.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Fully crossed G-theory design. Exp-001 evaluates MMLU across all facet levels; exp-002 extends to five benchmarks with reduced crossing. $^\dagger$BF16 balanced subset for cross-model analysis.}
\label{tab:design_matrix}
\small
\begin{tabular}{llrr}
\toprule
Facet & Levels & exp-001 & exp-002 \\
\midrule
Item ($p$) & Stratified sample & 200 & 200$\times$5 \\
Model ($M$) & 8 architecture families & 8 & 8 \\
Precision ($\pi$) & BF16 / FP16 / FP32 & 3 & 1 \\
Prompt ($\delta$) & Paraphrases & 6 & 6 \\
Seed ($s$) & Fixed set & 6 & 6 \\
Temperature ($\tau$) & 0.0 / 0.3 / 0.7 & 3 & 2 \\
Ordering ($o$) & Demo permutations & 4 & 4 \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{2}{l}{Total records} & 460,800$^\dagger$ & 2,304,000 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig2_design_diagram}
\caption{Fully crossed G-theory design for LLM evaluation. Each observation is a binary correctness score determined by the crossing of seven facets: item ($p$), model ($M$), precision ($\pi$), prompt ($\delta$), seed ($s$), temperature ($\tau$), and ordering ($o$).}
\label{fig:design_diagram}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Cross-Benchmark Extension}
\label{sec:exp002}

To test whether the variance structure observed on MMLU generalizes across evaluation formats, exp-002 extends the design to five benchmarks: MMLU, ARC-Challenge, and HellaSwag (multiple-choice), and GSM8K and MATH (free-form generation). The design crosses 8 models $\times$ BF16 $\times$ 2 temperatures (0.0, 0.7) $\times$ 6 prompts $\times$ 6 seeds $\times$ 4 orderings $\times$ 200 items per benchmark, totaling 2,304,000 records. This reduced crossing fixes precision at BF16 and uses two temperature levels to concentrate statistical power on format-associated differences. The ordering facet operates only on MMLU, where it selects among four permutations of the few-shot demonstration examples. For all other benchmarks, the prompt construction does not use the ordering parameter; ordering is structurally inert ($\sigma^2_{\text{ordering}} = 0$ by construction) and is retained solely for design balance. The two temperature levels bracket the most common evaluation configurations: deterministic ($\tau = 0.0$) and a moderate stochastic setting ($\tau = 0.7$) representative of practical deployment. A subset analysis confirms that this reduced crossing preserves the variance structure of the full design ($\Delta G = 0.0008$; Appendix~\ref{app:robustness}).


\subsection{Evaluation Protocol}
\label{sec:protocol}

All evaluations use generation-based scoring (free-text response with answer extraction) rather than log-likelihood ranking, following \citet{hua2025flaw}. Binary correctness (1/0) is the primary metric; text-exact-match is secondary. Inference uses vLLM~0.9.2 without batch-invariant kernels, preserving CUDA-level nondeterminism as a measurable variance source \cite{messina2026background, thinkingmachines2025batch}. Few-shot protocols: 5-shot MMLU/ARC, 4-shot MATH, 8-shot GSM8K, 10-shot HellaSwag.

\subsection{Binary Data Assumptions}
\label{sec:binary}

Standard G-theory assumes continuous, approximately normal outcome data \cite{shavelson1991generalizability}. Our binary correctness scores (0/1) violate this assumption. We establish robustness through simulation and estimator properties, with two additional analyses (GLMM complement, accuracy-range stability) in Appendix~\ref{app:robustness}.

A probit simulation matched to our 1,296-condition design confirms that Henderson Method~I recovers variance component rankings from dichotomized data (Spearman $\rho = 0.732$ between binary and continuous rankings; \texttt{item\_id} dominant in all 100 replications). Henderson Method~I produces unbiased estimates for balanced designs regardless of outcome distribution \cite[Ch.~12]{brennan2001generalizability}, and REML estimates differ by $<$0.5pp on all components (Appendix~\ref{app:robustness}).

Our target universe comprises open-weight, instruction-tuned models in the 7--9B parameter range, sampled from six architecture families. Results may not generalize to API-based models (where infrastructure is opaque), base (non-instruction-tuned) models, or models above 9B parameters. Treating eight purposively selected models as a quasi-random facet within this universe is validated by two analyses (Appendix~\ref{app:robustness}): four-model subset D-study projections predict $G = 0.893$ at eight models (observed: $G = 0.896$, $\Delta = 0.003$), and all eight leave-one-model-out subsets preserve component rankings.
\label{sec:model_facet}

GSM8K exhibits a fundamentally different variance profile. Item difficulty accounts for only 12\% of variance. Instead, model (15\%), prompt template (12\%), and model$\times$prompt interaction (9\%) become the leading components. The residual term absorbs 35\% of variance, indicating substantial unexplained variation from seed and temperature effects in free-form generation.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Format-conditional variance structure across four benchmarks (exp-002, 921,600 records). Top-5 components plus residual per benchmark. MC = multiple-choice, FF = free-form.}
\label{tab:cross_benchmark}
\small
\begin{tabular}{l rrrr}
\toprule
 & \multicolumn{3}{c}{MC} & FF \\
\cmidrule(lr){2-4} \cmidrule(lr){5-5}
Component & MMLU & ARC & Hella. & GSM8K \\
\midrule
\textbf{item\_id} & \textbf{42} & \textbf{49} & \textbf{45} & 12 \\
model$\times$item & 34 & 32 & 38 & 8 \\
\textbf{model} & 1 & 1 & 1 & \textbf{15} \\
prompt & $<$1 & $<$1 & $<$1 & 12 \\
model$\times$prompt & $<$1 & $<$1 & $<$1 & 9 \\
residual & 19 & 15 & 13 & 35 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
% [PLACEHOLDER: add MATH column + 8-model rows when data ready]
\end{table}

This dichotomy is not a continuous gradient. The gap between the lowest MC item dominance (MMLU, 42\%) and GSM8K (12\%) represents a qualitative shift in what drives evaluation variance. In multiple-choice settings, the item pool determines reliability; in free-form settings, prompt design and model selection dominate. Figure~\ref{fig:cross_benchmark} visualizes this structural contrast.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig1_cross_benchmark}
\caption{Variance decomposition by benchmark format. Multiple-choice benchmarks (MMLU, ARC, HellaSwag) share an item-dominated structure, while free-form GSM8K distributes variance across model, prompt, and interaction components.}
\label{fig:cross_benchmark}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Precision Separability}

Prior work has identified numerical precision as a potential source of evaluation variance \cite{yuan2025layercast}. Our multi-facet design allows us to isolate the precision contribution while controlling for all other facets simultaneously.

Under binary scoring with BF16 and FP16, the mean accuracy difference is 0.23 percentage points. After excluding FP32, the precision main effect drops to 0.0006\% of total variance. The FP32 anomaly traces to a vLLM implementation detail (\texttt{enforce\_eager} mode required for FP32 inference), not a fundamental precision effect.

Precision does not interact with other methodological facets under binary scoring: all precision$\times$methodology interactions contribute $<$0.1\% of variance. Under text-exact-match scoring, however, a precision$\times$temperature interaction emerges at 1.3\%, consistent with the metric-conditional pattern observed in Section~4.1. The practical implication is clear: for standard binary evaluation at BF16/FP16, numerical precision is a negligible variance source that need not be controlled. This finding is specific to single-GPU inference and does not address multi-GPU tensor parallelism, where numerical non-associativity introduces additional variance sources \cite{shanmugavelu2024floating}.

\subsection{Design-Dependent Reproducibility Budgets}

The previous sections establish that item selection and model$\times$item interaction dominate evaluation variance. A natural question follows: how many items does a given evaluation design need to achieve a target reliability? We address this through D-study projections that translate variance components into concrete item budgets.

Table~\ref{tab:dstudy} compares four evaluation designs at the $G \geq 0.80$ threshold. The multi-facet design (432 conditions per item: 3 precisions $\times$ 6 prompts $\times$ 6 seeds $\times$ 4 orderings) achieves $G = 0.918$ at 200 items and requires only 72 items for $G \geq 0.80$. A single-condition design (one fixed prompt, seed, ordering, and precision per item) reaches only $G = 0.559$ at 200 items and requires 631 items for the same threshold. The ratio between these budgets is 8.8$\times$, and this ratio remains stable across subset sizes from 50 to 200 items (CV = 1.9\%).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{D-study reproducibility budgets under different evaluation designs. The multi-facet design requires $8.8\times$ fewer items than single-condition evaluation to reach $G \geq 0.80$.}
\label{tab:dstudy}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcrrr}
\toprule
Design & Cond./item & $n_{G \geq 0.80}$ & $G_{200}$ & $G_{116}$ \\
\midrule
Multi-facet & 432 & \textbf{72} & \textbf{0.918} & \textbf{0.882} \\
Partial (4 cond.) & 4 & 189 & 0.810 & 0.721 \\
Single-condition & 1 & 631 & 0.559 & 0.424 \\
\midrule
IRT baseline & \multicolumn{1}{c}{---} & 116 & --- & 0.424$^\dagger$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\vspace{2pt}
{\scriptsize $^\dagger$\,PSN-IRT item count of 116 \cite{zhou2026irt} evaluated under single-condition protocol yields $G = 0.424$. See text for interpretation.}
\end{table}

The comparison with IRT-based efficient benchmarking is instructive. \citet{zhou2026irt} use IRT to select 116 informative items from MMLU, optimizing for item discrimination. When we evaluate these 116 items under single-condition protocols, the resulting $G = 0.424$ falls well below the reliability threshold. This is not a failure of IRT item selection; rather, it reflects that IRT optimizes a different objective (latent trait estimation) that does not account for methodological facet variance. Under the multi-facet design, 116 items yield $G = 0.882$, confirming that the reliability gap is driven by evaluation design, not item quality.

Figure~\ref{fig:dstudy} plots the D-study projection curves for each design. The multi-facet curve saturates rapidly (diminishing returns beyond $\sim$150 items), while the single-condition curve rises slowly and does not reach $G = 0.80$ within typical benchmark sizes.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig3_dstudy_curves}
\caption{D-study projection curves for three evaluation designs. The multi-facet design ($8.8\times$ fewer items) reaches $G \geq 0.80$ at 72 items; the single-condition design requires 631 items. The shaded band shows 95\% bootstrap CIs.}
\label{fig:dstudy}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Robustness Checks}

We conduct six robustness analyses to verify that the reported variance structures are not artifacts of specific analytical choices. \textbf{Item resampling} (exp-001b): three independent 200-item samples drawn from the full MMLU pool yield item difficulty variance shares of 59.8\%, 61.4\%, and 62.1\% (CV = 2.7\%) and $G_\text{item}$ values with CV = 0.5\%. \textbf{Estimator robustness}: REML and Henderson Method~I differ by $<$0.5 percentage points on all components. \textbf{CI method}: bootstrap and jackknife confidence intervals agree closely (width ratio 0.96--1.04 across components). \textbf{Facet specification}: treating facets as fixed vs.\ random changes $G_\text{item}$ by only 0.37 percentage points. \textbf{Leave-one-model-out (LOMO)}: all three-model subsets preserve the item + model$\times$item dominance pattern (item\_id 39--45\%, model$\times$item 32--37\%, $G_\text{item}$ 0.747--0.793). \textbf{Null calibration}: a Bernoulli simulation with matched item difficulties produces a variance ratio of 1.0008 relative to observed data, confirming that the decomposition does not introduce systematic bias on binary outcomes.

% [PLACEHOLDER: §4.7 Extended Results — 8-model × 5-benchmark expansion]

